<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/13th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Jaffa (1229)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1229">1229</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sixth-crusade">Sixth Crusade</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Jaffa of 1229 was an agreement between the Ayyubid sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-kamil">al-Kamil</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii">Holy Roman Emperor Frederick II</a>, by which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> was handed over to Christian control without a battle. Al-Kamil sought Frederick's political support against his own Ayyubid relatives. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire</a> describe the surrender of the city <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> had recovered as an ideological blow to the dynasty.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Frederick II and al-Kāmil had exchanged embassies in 1227, when, according to the Arabic chronicles, the sultan sought Frederick's help against his brother al-Muʿaẓẓam of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> and offered land in return. Al-Muʿaẓẓam died soon after, and when Frederick reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a> on 7 September 1228 the family dispute was unresolved. The two rulers never met: envoys passed between their camps, first Acre and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nablus">Nablus</a>, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffa">Jaffa</a> and Tall al-ʿAjūl. Al-Kāmil's chief negotiator was Fakhr al-Dīn, with whom Frederick could talk directly in Arabic, and the largely secret talks came with gifts and questions on philosophy and mathematics.</p><p>Frederick's position was weak: he was excommunicate, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-ix">Pope Gregory IX</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> during the talks, and his army could not take and hold <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. Al-Kāmil meanwhile agreed with his brother al-Ashraf on dividing the Ayyubid lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, and according to al-Maqrīzī he settled with Frederick because otherwise he could not enforce that bargain, which required a siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>. A draft was reached on 11 February 1229 and accepted by the sovereigns on 18 February.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>No complete copy survives, and the terms are reconstructed from Christian and Muslim writers who do not always agree. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> passed to the emperor with a corridor to the coast, including the diocese of Lydda, while the Ḥaram al-Sharīf stayed in Muslim hands with a Muslim garrison and judge, open to pilgrims of both faiths; other Muslims, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, were to leave the city. Christian possession of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffa">Jaffa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazareth">Nazareth</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidon">Sidon</a> and Toron was recognised, and a truce of ten years, five months and forty days was agreed. The sources differ on whether Frederick could rebuild the walls.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Frederick entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> on 17 March and was crowned in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-holy-sepulchre">Church of the Holy Sepulchre</a> the next day, after which the city was placed under interdict because an excommunicate had negotiated the treaty. In besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, al-Nāṣir Dāʾūd had Sibṭ ibn al-Jawzī denounce the treaty in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-mosque">Umayyad Mosque</a>, while al-Kāmil argued that he had conceded only some churches and ruined houses. Frederick's poets Freidank and Guillem Figueira praised the recovery, but the church hierarchy and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-templar">Templars</a> and Hospitallers, who had not been consulted, were angered. Jerusalem was not refortified during the truce.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Jaffa (1229)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Al-Kamil ceded Jerusalem to the Holy Roman Emperor Frederick II by diplomacy in the <b>Treaty of Jaffa (1229)</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>During his reign the Ayyubids defeated the Fifth Crusade, and in 1229 he surrendered Jerusalem to the emperor Frederick II under the <b>Treaty of Jaffa</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-31">31 August in history</a></li></ul></section>
